inal lingering thought to mull over today. We started this entire journey by talking about x-rays. We talked about how much we crave looking for that clean, visible, perfectly manageable line of a broken bone. We want it to be simple. In many ways, we built our entire modern digital world trying to artificially create that clean, visible, manageable space. We built the internet as a frictionless virtual escape from the messy, muddy, resistant waters of the physical world. But maybe that was the wrong goal. Right. Because if digital systems are so inherently fragile that they desperately need human quadrangulation hard-coded into them just to not lose their minds, and if the ultimate, most elegant, most robust computer Flixank can imagine isn't a quantum microchip, but is simply a physical house regulating its own temperature through a wall of melting wax. Then what does that say about the physical world? Then maybe the ultimate goal of our technology shouldn't be to build a separate, frictionless virtual reality to escape into. Maybe the highest, most profound form of computation we can aspire to is simply the act of containing, maintaining, and surviving within the beautiful, friction-filled physical world we already inhabit.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 7.34, "text": " You know, usually when we talk about getting a medical diagnosis, there is this comforting expectation of absolute precision.", "tokens": [50365, 509, 458, 11, 2673, 562, 321, 751, 466, 1242, 257, 4625, 15217, 11, 456, 307, 341, 38439, 14334, 295, 8236, 18356, 13, 50732], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11736408112541077, "compression_ratio": 1.6423611111111112, "no_speech_prob": 1.090034529652173e-12}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 7.8, "end": 9.56, "text": " Right. Yeah. Like it's a math problem.", "tokens": [50755, 1779, 13, 865, 13, 1743, 309, 311, 257, 5221, 1154, 13, 50843], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11736408112541077, "compression_ratio": 1.642361